    ['Counseling', 'Which reversible method has the LOWEST typical-use failure rate?',
      ['Etonogestrel implant', 'DMPA injection', 'Combined oral contraceptive pill', 'Vaginal ring', 'Male condoms'],
      'Implant: about 0.1% per year, the most effective method. IUDs: 0.1\u20130.8%. DMPA: about 4%. Pill, patch and ring: about 7%. Condoms: about 13%. Long-acting methods take user error out of the equation.'],
    ['Management', 'She mentions unprotected intercourse 4 days ago. Which emergency contraception option is MOST effective?',
      ['Copper IUD insertion today', 'Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg orally', 'Ulipristal acetate 30 mg orally', 'Two combined pills taken 12 hours apart (Yuzpe method)', 'Nothing is effective after 72 hours'],
      'A copper IUD within 5 days is the most effective emergency contraception (pregnancy rate below 0.1%) and also provides ongoing contraception. (US SPR 2024 also lists the LNG 52-mg IUD.) Ulipristal works up to 120 hours. Oral levonorgestrel is labeled for 72 hours and is less effective, especially with higher BMI.'],
    ['Counseling', 'Which medication most clearly REDUCES the effectiveness of combined oral contraceptives?',
      ['Rifampin', 'Amoxicillin', 'Levetiracetam', 'Sertraline', 'Metformin'],
      'Strong CYP3A4 inducers (rifampin, rifabutin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, topiramate, some antiretrovirals, St John\u2019s wort) lower contraceptive steroid levels. Most broad-spectrum antibiotics do NOT. For patients on enzyme inducers, IUDs or DMPA are good choices.'],
    ['Counseling', 'If she chooses DMPA (the injection), what counseling is accurate?',
      ['It can cause reversible bone mineral density loss and a delayed return to fertility after stopping', 'It has the same VTE risk as estrogen-containing pills', 'It causes permanent infertility', 'It must be given monthly', 'It increases the risk of endometrial cancer'],
      'DMPA is given every 13 weeks (up to 15). It has a boxed warning for BMD loss, which recovers after stopping. Return to fertility can take up to 10 months or more. Irregular bleeding and weight gain are common. It REDUCES endometrial cancer risk.']
